    "result_text": "All tests pass. Here's what I changed to satisfy the spec with minimal structural impact:\n\n**New behaviour, isolated in one small class** (`mapper/MembershipLevel.java`):\n- `of(namesakeCount, email)` \u2192 starts at 1, +1 for an email, +1 when `namesakeCount == 0`, capped at 3. WMC_other is 0 (single method), CC is low.\n\n**Four one-liner-scale edits:**\n- `OwnerMapper.toOwnerDto` \u2014 mapped `membershipLevel` (via `MembershipLevel.of`) in place of `membershipTier`.\n- `openapi.yml` \u2014 replaced the `membershipTier` string schema with a `membershipLevel` integer (min 1, example 3), so the generated DTO now exposes `membershipLevel` and drops `membershipTier`.\n- `RespondWithOwner` / `RespondWithOwnerCreated` \u2014 removed the GOLD-tier override blocks that set the now-removed field. Each is a single-method class (no surrounding complexity).\n\n**Notes:**\n- The create audit line (`SaveOwner`) logs id/customerCode/registrationDate and never recorded the tier, so nothing there needed to change \u2014 the \"record the level instead\" rule only bites where the tier was actually recorded.\n- The household `GOLD` string tier is gone (level 4 is reserved for tenure, and the level is capped at 3), so the old `HouseholdTier`/`MembershipTier` helpers are no longer called. I left them untouched rather than delete them, to keep the number of files touched \u2014 the dominant term in the impact cost \u2014 as low as possible; they're inert utility classes.\n\nThe acceptance test (`membershipLevel == 3` for a unique owner with an email) passes, and the full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`).",
